        passageEn:
            "For this week's history project, Nova School arranged a video call with Mrs Eleanor Hughes, an elderly guest from Britain who had been evacuated as a child during World War II. Su Su prepared her questions carefully, using everything she had learned about evacuation, the Blitz and rationing, and hoped Mrs Hughes could tell her what it had really felt like.\n\nOn the call, Mrs Hughes described being just seven years old, standing on a crowded railway platform in London with a gas mask box on a string around her neck and a name label pinned to her coat. She did not even know which town the train was taking her to. She was billeted with a kind farming family in the countryside, who let her help feed the chickens and taught her how to milk a cow, though she missed her parents terribly. On visits back to London, she remembered lying awake listening to the distant thud of bombs falling during the Blitz, and feeling grateful for the blackout curtains and the Anderson shelter in the garden.\n\nSu Su thanked Mrs Hughes and closed her laptop slowly, her notebook full of new notes. She had read about evacuation in books, but hearing it from someone who had truly lived through it made the whole topic feel different, more real and more human. \"An eyewitness can tell you things no textbook ever could,\" Su Su wrote at the top of her history journal that evening.",
