        passageEn:
            "For her Term 9 project proposal, Su Su decided to place her Term 4 river fieldwork report next to her Term 5 climate and biome world map, side by side on her desk, to see what she could learn from comparing them. Her river report traced the Ayeyarwady from a mountain source, through a winding middle course full of meanders, down to a wide delta where the river finally met the sea, depositing sediment the whole way. She remembered kneeling by a stream near school, timing how fast a leaf floated downstream to estimate the water's speed.\n\nHer climate and biome map told a different kind of story, but on the very same river. Su Su had marked Myanmar's position on the world climate map, close enough to the equator to fall within a tropical climate zone, and had compared it to a tundra biome near the poles, where the ground stayed frozen for most of the year. She noted that a tropical river like the Ayeyarwady rarely froze, while a river starting in a tundra biome might stay solid ice for months at a time.\n\nPlacing the two projects together, Su Su realised a river's whole journey -- its speed, its temperature, even whether it ever freezes -- depended enormously on the climate zone and biome it flowed through. A geography fact from Term 4 and a geography fact from Term 5 were not really separate lessons at all, she decided; they were two different lenses for looking at the very same planet.",
        passageMy:
