            "On the final day of the term, the teacher gives Moe Moe, Nay Chi, and Htet Htet a big piece of paper and asks them to make a map of their classroom together.\n\nMoe Moe carefully draws small squares for the desks and a rectangle for the door, remembering how maps show real objects from a bird's-eye view.\n\nNay Chi adds a map key in the corner, drawing tiny symbols and writing what each one means, just like the map key she made for her own classroom drawing.\n\nHtet Htet draws a small arrow pointing toward the window where the morning sun comes in, labeling it \"East,\" since that is the direction the sun rises.\n\nWhen they finish, the three friends stand back and admire their map, seeing their whole classroom, with its desks, door, window, and directions, captured on one page. Their teacher says their map is just like the maps that show cities, farms, and forests across Myanmar, only much smaller. The three friends feel proud, knowing that whether it is a tiny classroom or their whole country, they now understand how maps and places connect.",
