        passageEn:
            "In class, Su Su's teacher showed a photograph unlike anything she had ever seen: a wide, flat plain dotted with thousands of ancient brick temples stretching all the way to the horizon. \"This is Bagan,\" the teacher explained, \"built by one of Myanmar's earliest and most powerful kingdoms.\"\n\n"
            "\"Thousands of temples?\" Su Su asked in disbelief. \"Kings and wealthy families built them, one by one, over hundreds of years,\" the teacher said. \"Each temple was a way of showing devotion and leaving something lasting behind.\"\n\n"
            "The teacher explained that many of these temples still stand today, protected and preserved so that people can visit and study them, hundreds and hundreds of years after they were built. \"Historians study the temples and old writings to learn what life was like in the ancient kingdom,\" she said, \"since much of it was never written down in the way we write things today.\"\n\n"
            "Su Su stared at the photograph, trying to imagine the kingdom that had built such an enormous field of temples so long ago. \"It's amazing that we can still see it,\" she said quietly. \"That's why preserving old places matters so much,\" the teacher replied. \"Without them, this whole chapter of Myanmar's history could have been lost forever.\"",
