        titleMy: "ကောင်းစက်၏ ပထမဆုံး ရေပွဲတော်",
        passageEn:
            "For the first time, Kaung Sett was old enough to join the Thingyan celebrations in the streets, and he could hardly sleep the night before. \"Tomorrow, everyone celebrates the New Year together,\" his mother reminded him.\n\n"
            "The next morning, the streets exploded with laughter as neighbours splashed water at each other from buckets, hoses, and water guns. Kaung Sett got soaked within minutes, laughing as Su Su chased him with a bucket of cold water.\n\n"
            "Later that afternoon, his family visited the monastery, where the mood turned calm and quiet. Kaung Sett watched his mother gently pour water over his grandmother's hands as a sign of respect, then helped wash a small Buddha image, careful and slow where the streets had been loud and fast.\n\n"
            "That evening, tired and happy, Kaung Sett thought about the two very different sides of the same day. \"This morning was wild and loud,\" he said to his grandmother, \"but this afternoon felt so peaceful.\" His grandmother smiled. \"Thingyan has room for both,\" she said. \"The joyful splashing washes away the old year, and the quiet blessings welcome the new one with respect.\"",
        passageMy:
